            0.087
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part I"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.115,
            0.398,
            0.138
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.152,
            0.872,
            0.21
          ],
          "content": "Why does a negative times a negative equal a positive? Think of it in terms of action and debt. If a negative number represents a debt, then multiplying by a negative number represents the removal of that debt."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.213,
            0.872,
            0.272
          ],
          "content": "(**Hint:** If someone takes away (–) four of your debts that are each worth \\`3 (that is, –3), you are effectively \\`12 richer! Therefore, (–3) × (– 4) = +12.)"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.374,
            0.31,
            0.622,
            0.34
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Exercise Set 3.2"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.352,
            0.885,
            0.412
          ],
          "content": "1. The temperature in the high-altitude desert of Ladakh is recorded as $4   ^ { \\circ } \\mathsf { C }$ at noon. By midnight, it drops by $1 5 \\; ^ { \\circ } \\mathrm { C } ,$ What is the midnight temperature?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.415,
            0.886,
            0.497
          ],
          "content": "2. A spice trader takes a loan (debt) of \\`850. The next day, he makes a profit (fortune) of \\`1,200. The following week, he incurs a loss of \\`450. Write this sequence as an equation using integers and calculate his final financial standing."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.499,
            0.717,
            0.521
          ],
          "content": "3. Calculate the following using Brahmagupta’s laws:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.181,
            0.526,
            0.326,
            0.548
          ],
          "content": "(i) $( - 1 2 ) \\times 5$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.548,
            0.526,
            0.709,
            0.548
          ],
          "content": "(ii) $(-8) \\times (-7)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.56,
            0.325,
            0.582
          ],
          "content": "(iii) 0 – (–14)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.548,
            0.561,
            0.7,
            0.582
          ],
          "content": "(iv) $(-20) \\div \\; 4$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.591,
            0.886,
            0.652
          ],
          "content": "4. Explain, using a real-world example of debt, why subtracting a negative number is the same as adding a positive number (e.g., 10 – (–5) = 15)."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.675,
            0.787,
            0.723
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "3.4 Filling the Spaces: Fractions and Rational Numbers"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.734,
            0.886,
            0.811
          ],
          "content": "As society grew more complex, measuring became just as important as counting. If a farmer divides a field of wheat among his three children, how much does each get? If a recipe calls for half a cup of ghee, how do we represent that mathematically?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.81,
            0.884,
            0.851
          ],
          "content": "Numbers that represent parts of a whole are called **fractions**. Just as every natural number has an additive inverse (3 has –3, 19 has –19, etc.),"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.855,
            0.885,
            0.913
          ],
          "content": "we can conceive of additive inverses for every positive fraction: $-\\frac{3}{4}$ for ${ \\frac { 3 } { 4 } }   ,   -   { \\frac { 1 9 } { 7 } }$ for , etc. Let us refer to such additive inverses of positive $\\frac { 1 9 } { 7 }$"
        }
      ]
    }
